  "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B009",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and experimental development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B022",
    "B013"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the most compelling experimentally mature measurement advance on the shortlist: the magic-wavelength principle addresses the defining lattice-induced systematic while retaining the signal advantages of neutral-atom ensembles. Katori's conceptual contribution and Ye's decisive experimental development form a compact, complementary attribution. The essential physics has been reproduced quantitatively in independent laboratories over many years; recognition need not rest on a future redefinition of the second or still-developing applications.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary remains important. A citation must recognize the lattice-clock architecture and its foundational experimental development, not absorb the broader optical-frequency-metrology enterprise or later technical refinements. Before a final award, archival review should test whether another early contributor has a comparably indispensable claim. I also recognize that atom interferometry and photonic crystals are major mature optical achievements, though their foundational lineages and credit boundaries are less sharply bounded on the supplied record.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that neodymium-iron-boron magnets are a mature, compactly attributed breakthrough with durable physical and technological consequences, but questions whether the achievement is fundamentally physics rather than materials engineering.",
        "response": "I agree that the magnets have exceptional durability and that Sagawa and Croat may prove a compact attribution after historical review. The unresolved classification is nevertheless material: B009 identifies a new atomic-measurement principle whose central systematic limitation was overcome and whose precision has been independently demonstrated. That direct connection between physical principle, controlled experiment, and reproducible capability gives B009 the stronger case for this prize."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors quantum error correction and topological fault tolerance as foundational principles, while acknowledging that useful-scale fully fault-tolerant quantum computation has not yet been demonstrated.",
        "response": "I share the judgment that error correction is a profound response to decoherence and a necessary framework for scalable quantum information. But the joint B021 formulation also compresses broad error correction and a distinct topological route, with many consequential contributors. In contrast, B009 has a narrower achievement and an already repeated experimental realization of its defining promise; this difference in both maturity and attribution keeps it ahead."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth supports B009 but stresses that the original lattice-clock architecture must be separated from later community-wide optical-metrology advances.",
        "response": "I agree fully and retain the same narrow framing. The case for Katori and Ye is strongest when it is based on the magic-wavelength architecture and defining experimental development, followed by independent confirmation, rather than on claiming all present optical-clock performance or prospective applications. This caution does not weaken the central achievement; it makes its credit assignment more defensible."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether primary historical evidence supports limiting credit for the original optical-lattice-clock architecture to Katori and Ye without omitting a comparably indispensable contributor.",
      "How precisely the citation can distinguish the lattice-clock invention and its foundational experimental development from broader optical-frequency metrology and later refinements.",
      "Whether the original Nd-Fe-B developments can be shown to meet the Physics Prize threshold as a fundamental materials discovery rather than principally an industrial engineering achievement."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No recusal-level connection to the proposed laureates is apparent from the supplied profile. My work in atomic physics and quantum optics gives relevant subject expertise but no stated direct collaboration or institutional connection to Katori or Ye."
  }
}
